h1('ABSTRACT')
p('AI Software Tester is a locally operated quality assurance platform that connects natural language requirements with generated test cases, controlled automation, execution evidence, failure analysis and defect records. It combines a Streamlit interface, Python services, a SQLite database, an Ollama-hosted language model, Playwright browser automation and an HTTP API test engine. Its central design principle is that model output is a proposal: structured response parsing and an explicit action allow-list stand between generated instructions and browser execution. Testers can review generated artifacts, run tests against a configured target, retain outcomes and screenshots, and use dashboard and report views to inspect coverage and failures. The repository contains a Flask demonstration target with four deliberately documented defects, and pytest suites for AI response parsing, database operations, action validation and metrics. This report follows the supplied project outline and expands each section with system context, design rationale, data model, operational flow, implementation detail, verification scope, limitations and future work. Local-first execution can avoid routine external inference requests after software and model assets are acquired; it does not prove that every deployment is secure or that AI-generated tests are complete. The current code and project materials use Qwen2.5-Coder 7B as the default model. Static source inventory identifies 47 test functions, while an existing project narrative records 46 passing tests; those counts should be reconciled against a fresh run before formal submission. The project demonstrates a practical, reviewable workflow for AI-assisted QA while leaving test acceptance, environment configuration and defect disposition under human control.')
h2('Report Scope and Reading Guide')
p('The report describes the project as represented by checked-in code and accompanying materials. Chapters 1 and 2 explain the problem domain and system objectives. Chapter 3 records the development environment and requirements. Chapter 4 describes modules, input and output design, database structure and information flow. Chapter 5 presents testing and implementation evidence. Chapter 6 summarizes the outcome and staged enhancements; Chapter 7 lists technical references. Appendices provide a screen inventory and export guidance. The report distinguishes documented capabilities from recommendations and identifies discrepancies where the code and narrative differ.')
