h1('1. INTRODUCTION')
for x in [
'Quality assurance starts before a browser is opened. Teams interpret requirements, identify observable behavior, select representative data, define expected results and decide how failures can be reproduced. Automation adds another layer: test authors translate those decisions into scripts, maintain selectors and fixtures, control environments and preserve links between requirement versions and execution outcomes. When activities are split across documents and tools, the effort to keep them aligned grows with the application and with each release.',
'AI Software Tester brings several of these tasks into one local application. A tester creates a project and target base URL, enters requirements in natural language, requests a model-assisted analysis, generates candidate test cases, reviews them, and can request browser or API actions. The application validates browser actions before Playwright executes them. Results, timing, failure descriptions, identifiers and screenshots can be stored and surfaced through dashboards, bug management and reports.',
'The model is integrated as a service rather than an autonomous operator. Prompt templates request structured output for requirement analysis, test generation, action generation, failure analysis and bug drafting. A response parser extracts and validates JSON, and the AI service retries malformed output up to three times. This improves predictability but cannot ensure that an interpretation is correct. Human review remains essential for security-sensitive cases, generated data and defect severity.',
'The platform is implemented primarily in Python. Streamlit provides the user interface; SQLite stores project data; Ollama serves a local model; Playwright supports Chromium, Firefox and WebKit; and a requests-based engine performs API tests. A Flask sample application provides a controlled target with seeded behaviors. This lets the project demonstrate navigation, validation, display and authentication tests without depending on a third-party application.',
'This report expands the supplied outline without changing its major sections or named subsections. It describes the system as represented in the repository. Assertions in the prior narrative are not treated as proof of a current live run. The differing model defaults and test counts are recorded so they can be reconciled before formal use.'
]:p(x)
for x in [
'The project has several linked objectives. It aims to shorten the time between understanding a requirement and preparing an executable test, preserve a persistent relationship between requirement and result, and make failures easier to inspect with common evidence fields. A further objective is to keep model-suggested actions inside a narrow execution interface. These objectives should be evaluated separately: generation speed does not prove correctness, traceability does not prove coverage, and a safe action list does not prove that the test data or target is safe.',
'The intended primary user is a tester or developer working on a web application who can create a project, run local services and review test outcomes. Product analysts can contribute requirement descriptions, but a technical operator is needed to install the model runtime and browser binaries. The system is most useful when the application has stable pages or APIs that can be exercised repeatedly and when expected behavior is documented sufficiently for assertions.',
'The scope includes functional and selected non-functional checks that can be expressed through the supported browser and API actions. The current design is not a replacement for load testing, formal security assessment, accessibility auditing or exploratory testing. Nor does a generated test automatically become an approved acceptance criterion. Those activities may inform test creation but require their own tools, expertise and review.',
'The main workflow has four kinds of information. Intent is expressed by requirements and priorities. Verification design is represented by cases, steps, data and expected results. Execution evidence records run settings, status, timing and observed errors. Disposition records analysis and bugs. Keeping these categories distinct helps a reviewer see whether a defect arises from a mistaken requirement, weak test, automation problem or product behavior.',
'For a useful result, requirements should be decomposed into observable statements. “The system is secure” is too broad to assert directly; a testable statement would identify the actor, resource, authorization rule and expected denial behavior. AI analysis can suggest such questions, but it cannot supply missing business policy. The tester should return unresolved assumptions to the requirement owner instead of allowing the model to invent a rule.',
'The application follows a local-first operational model. Local does not mean isolated from all networks: installation downloads dependencies, the target may be remote, and the Ollama endpoint is configurable. The data file and screenshots reside on the workstation. An organization should inventory those routes and files before classifying the system for confidential work.',
'The report also treats the project as a software engineering artifact. Its quality depends on the clarity of module boundaries, validation contracts, database behavior, maintainability and reproducible verification. The interface is only one part of that quality. A professional evaluation should inspect the code path from prompt construction to stored result and should reproduce representative cases against the demo application.',
'The intended outcome is an assistive test workbench, not autonomous acceptance. A human decides which requirement interpretation is valid, which generated scenarios belong in a suite, which target is authorized, whether a failure is a product defect and how a bug should be prioritized. This allocation of responsibility is important because language models can generate plausible but unsupported details.'
]:p(x)
