h1('2. SYSTEM STUDY AND ANALYSIS')
h2('2.1 Problem statement')
for x in [
'Manual test design can be thorough, but it consumes time and depends on a reviewer’s experience, context and attention. A short requirement may omit data constraints, error handling or authorization expectations. If testers interpret it differently, cases become inconsistent. When a team automates the cases, it must encode the intent again in scripts and assertions, often in another repository or tool.',
'This separation creates practical risks. A test may no longer match its requirement after a change. A defect record may not identify the run that exposed it. A failure may lack the URL, browser, test data or screenshot needed to reproduce it. Teams can spend time classifying infrastructure and selector failures as product defects, while real defects remain hidden among noisy results.',
'The project addresses the need for an integrated workflow that accepts plain-language requirements, proposes structured analyses and cases, converts selected cases into a limited set of actions, runs those actions, stores evidence and assists with interpretation. The design must preserve the link between source requirement and result while controlling the transition from probabilistic model output to real browser actions.',
'The problem is broader than generating test text. A useful system must handle input quality, response structure, action validation, execution errors, persistence, traceability, review and reporting. If the model is unavailable or returns invalid content, the interface should show the error rather than silently substituting an unrelated model or representing a test as successful.'
]:p(x)
for x in [
'From a requirements-engineering perspective, the quality problem begins with incomplete, ambiguous or conflicting statements. A requirement may describe a goal without specifying the actor, trigger, precondition, observable response or failure behavior. A test tool can faithfully store ambiguous text, and an AI model can make it sound more precise, yet neither action resolves which interpretation stakeholders intended. The workflow therefore needs a visible distinction between extracted information and questions that remain open.',
'The project’s core user need is to reduce the repetitive translation work while preserving review. A requirement owner should be able to recognize the generated scenarios as faithful; a tester should be able to inspect steps and assertions; and a developer should receive failure context sufficient to reproduce a defect. Each role needs different details, so reports should avoid presenting a single pass-rate value as a complete quality judgment.',
'An effective system must also handle uncertainty at each stage. Generated test cases are hypotheses about useful coverage. Generated actions are mappings from those cases to an interface. Execution outcomes are observations from one environment and one point in time. AI failure analysis is an interpretation of selected evidence. None of these should be silently promoted to verified fact. Provenance and status labels make that uncertainty manageable.',
'Several constraints bound the proposed scope. The project targets a local single-user workflow; the visible schema does not include shared-user permissions. The browser engine implements a fixed set of actions; it does not support arbitrary JavaScript, drag-and-drop or file-upload operations. API assertions cover selected response properties rather than a complete contract language. These limits are useful for a prototype but should be explicit when selecting a target application.',
'The quality goal is therefore practical repeatability. Given the same approved case, target build, browser and data setup, the runner should perform the same defined actions, report a comparable result and retain enough context for follow-up. Exact runtime may vary, but the expected behavior and failure classification path should be stable. Repeating a run is meaningful only if the environment and state are controlled.'
]:p(x)
for x in [
'Functional requirements can be grouped into user-visible capabilities. Project management should create a project with a base URL. Requirement management should attach a natural-language description and priority. AI analysis should return structured findings or a visible error. Test generation should produce reviewable cases connected to the requirement. Case management should allow edits and manual additions. Execution should run selected cases and persist outcomes. Bug management should preserve reproduction detail and status. Reporting should export a selected project view.',
'Cross-cutting requirements concern integrity and usability. The interface should identify the active project and runtime configuration; generated content should remain editable; the user should be able to distinguish an AI proposal from an approved record; and execution should return a clear result even when a dependency fails. The database should preserve links between entities and avoid accidental duplicate identifiers. These are design targets that can guide review even when they are not each covered by an automated test.',
'Non-functional requirements include local responsiveness, diagnosable errors, reproducible setup, acceptable inference delay, safe target selection and traceable records. A prototype does not need enterprise throughput, but users should know when a model call or browser run is still active. Persisted data should survive restart, while temporary demo data should be removable. Reports should remain readable and should not expose secrets.',
'Acceptance criteria should be observable. For example, creating a requirement should make it appear in the selected project; a valid model response should produce an editable analysis; an unsupported action should be rejected before launch; a completed case should have a result tied to its run; and a bug created from a failure should retain its relation to that result. These criteria connect architecture to verifiable behavior.',
'Usability review should focus on reducing context switching. A tester should be able to move from a requirement to its test cases, see whether they have been executed, open the result evidence and create a linked bug. If identifiers or links are hidden, the underlying relational schema provides less practical value. Navigation should make the traceability chain visible and avoid forcing users to manually copy IDs.',
'Error messages should state what the user can do next. “Ollama unavailable” can suggest checking the service and endpoint. “Invalid JSON after retries” can expose a safe excerpt and allow retry or manual entry. “Browser not installed” should point to the dependency setup. “Blocked: no actions defined” should link to the case editor. Actionable errors reduce support effort and prevent misclassification.'
]:p(x)
h2('2.2 Existing system')
for x in [
'In a conventional workflow, a requirement is captured in a specification or issue tracker. A test engineer clarifies ambiguity, builds scenarios and records expected behavior. Cases may live in a spreadsheet or test-management product. Automation scripts are written separately using a browser framework. A continuous-integration system may execute the scripts, while engineers inspect logs, browser traces and screenshots.',
'This arrangement can work well when ownership and tooling are clear. Dedicated test-management systems may offer mature planning and access control, while browser frameworks provide direct application control. The challenge is coordination: each artifact can be sound while the relationship between requirement, case, script, run and bug remains informal. Renaming a test or changing an expected value may leave a separate automation script unchanged.',
'Record-and-playback utilities address some scripting effort by capturing interactions. Their output can help with simple paths, but recorded steps encode a page state and data set. They do not resolve ambiguous requirements, select boundary cases or explain whether an assertion failure indicates a product defect or an automation problem.',
'Cloud AI services can offer large-scale inference and hosted collaboration. They may also require sending requirement text, application details or test data to an external service. Adoption depends on data rules, vendor terms, availability and recurring cost. A local approach avoids routine external inference requests when correctly configured, while making the team responsible for model and workstation resources.'
]:p(x)
h3('2.2.1 Drawbacks')
for x in [
'Manual translation is repeated work. Every new feature or changed behavior may require revisiting a specification, enumerating scenarios, writing steps and encoding expected outcomes. Regression cycles repeat much of the effort, while time pressure often favors happy paths over less common conditions.',
'Coverage depends on individual judgment. Boundary values, malformed input, empty states, authorization, state transitions and accessibility are easy to miss without a shared checklist. A large case count can still leave important behaviors unverified; mapping tests to requirements and risks is more informative.',
'Scripts and requirements can drift. If cases live in one system and automation in another, links may depend on naming conventions or manual references. Refactoring a selector or changing an expected value may not update the source requirement record. Auditing what a run verified becomes harder.',
'Failure diagnosis is often manual. A failure may originate in product behavior, a stale locator, expired session, unavailable environment, missing data or transient network condition. Without consistent context and evidence, engineers reconstruct events from partial logs. This increases triage time and false bug reports.',
'Cloud inference adds data-handling and availability considerations. A team may be restricted from sharing specifications or data with a hosted model. Network latency and service limits can interrupt work, and per-request pricing can make repeated generation costly. These tradeoffs differ by organization.',
'Automated generation has its own risks. A fluent case can misunderstand a rule, invent an endpoint or assert unsupported behavior. Guessed selectors can be brittle. Without schema checks, review and execution boundaries, automation may amplify errors rather than reduce effort.'
]:p(x)
h2('2.3 Proposed system')
for x in [
'The proposed system combines requirement capture, AI-assisted interpretation, test generation, action planning, controlled execution and defect documentation in one Streamlit application. Projects store a target base URL and related requirements. The AI service sends prompt templates to Ollama and expects JSON-shaped responses. A parser extracts JSON and checks expected fields. Invalid output may be retried with a correction instruction, up to the configured limit.',
'After a case is reviewed, the model can propose UI actions or an API test. Browser actions pass through the same validator at generation time and again in the Playwright engine before execution. The allow-list limits action categories and checks required fields. It prevents unsupported action types from reaching the executor; it does not replace authorization, URL scope controls, selector review or careful secret handling.',
'The executor distinguishes API and UI cases. The API engine submits an allowed method and evaluates status, response time, selected JSON fields and headers. The browser engine launches a configured browser, creates a context and page, then processes actions in order. The first failed action returns a failure record and can trigger a screenshot. The test executor persists the result against the run and case.',
'Failure analysis and bug generation are assistance steps. A tester reviews classification and suggested investigation, edits a draft report and decides whether it represents a product defect. The database stores projects, requirements, test cases, runs, results and bugs in related tables, allowing project-level metrics and traceability.',
'The workflow can be summarized as create project, add requirement, analyze, generate cases, review and approve, generate actions, validate, execute, analyze failures, create bugs and export reports. It is feedback-aware: cases, prompts and action definitions can be corrected and rerun after diagnosis.'
]:p(x)
h3('2.3.1 Advantages')
for a,b in [
('Reduced setup friction','Start from requirement text instead of writing every automation artifact from scratch; generated content remains editable.'),
('Repeatable breadth prompts','Prompts explicitly consider negative, boundary, validation and security behavior, subject to human confirmation.'),
('One local workflow','Requirement, case, run, analysis and defect records share a project context and identifiers.'),
('Constrained execution','Browser actions are selected from a small allow-list and checked before execution.'),
('Useful evidence','Duration, error, failed step and URL can accompany a screenshot to aid reproduction.'),
('Simple deployment','SQLite avoids a separate server, and Streamlit provides an interactive UI with little custom frontend code.'),
('Local inference option','A local model can keep prompt contents within the local environment when configured accordingly.'),
('Faster triage starting point','Structured failure analysis can suggest categories and next checks, subject to review.'),
('Portable reports','HTML and CSV support sharing and analysis; PDF can provide a stable snapshot when enabled.'),
('Demonstrable behavior','A demo target with known defects provides visible examples of functional and security failures.'),
('Modular foundation','Prompt, data, page and execution responsibilities are divided into packages.')]:bullet(a,b)
p('These advantages describe the workflow, not guaranteed defect-detection superiority. Generated coverage should be compared with a risk-based test plan, and the model should be evaluated on representative requirements before it becomes a quality gate.')
h2('2.4 Feasibility Analysis')
for x in [
'Technical feasibility is supported by a mature Python ecosystem and modular repository. Streamlit handles the UI; SQLite provides transactional persistence; Playwright exposes browser automation; requests supports HTTP operations; and Ollama offers a local inference endpoint. Constraints include workstation memory, model latency, browser installation, target availability and schema compatibility as the product evolves.',
'Hardware feasibility depends on model size and quantization. The project material specifies 16 GB RAM for Qwen2.5-Coder 7B and describes GPU acceleration as optional. Treat this as a planning estimate, not a benchmark: model build, context length and other running applications affect memory. The checked-in config currently names a 3B model, so operators must verify the active tag rather than assume 7B.',
'Economic feasibility is favorable for a single-user prototype because core components are open-source or locally available and local inference has no per-call cloud charge. Costs remain in hardware, electricity, setup, model storage and staff time. Local inference can be slower or less capable than hosted alternatives, and prompt curation requires effort.',
'Operational feasibility is helped by a browser-based interface and guided forms. Setup still requires Python packages, browser binaries and a running model. The workflow is most practical for developers and QA engineers who can configure services and diagnose target failures.',
'Privacy feasibility improves with local inference and persistence but depends on deployment choices. Target traffic may reach a remote system. Screenshots, API responses and prompt logs can contain sensitive information on disk. Define retention, access, masking and backup rules before using production data.',
'Schedule feasibility is appropriate for a mini-project because established libraries and a small local target keep the scope manageable. A successful demo does not prove compatibility with arbitrary web architectures, authentication schemes or APIs; it demonstrates bounded feasibility.'
]:p(x)

h1('3. DEVELOPMENT ENVIRONMENT')
p('The project is designed for a workstation where the UI, database, AI runtime and test runner operate as local services. The repository separates UI pages, AI integration, data access and execution engines, but they share one Python environment and a SQLite database. This makes setup compact and inspection straightforward; dependencies and configuration must remain consistent.')
h2('3.1 Hardware Requirement')
p('The original project material gives a quad-core processor at 2.0 GHz or higher, 16 GB RAM, 10 GB free storage and an optional GPU. These are planning values, especially for a 7B model, rather than guaranteed performance specifications. Model weights, runtime memory, context length, browser processes, screenshots and concurrent applications affect actual use. GPU acceleration depends on runtime and drivers; CPU-only operation may be slower.')
tab(['Component','Project baseline','Engineering note'],[
('Processor','Quad-core CPU, 2.0 GHz+','Suitable baseline for UI and light browser runs; inference depends on CPU generation.'),('Memory','16 GB RAM','Planning minimum for described local model; verify simultaneous model and browser usage.'),('Storage','10 GB free','Allow additional room for weights, Python, browser binaries, database, reports and screenshots.'),('Graphics','Optional GPU','Acceleration depends on supported drivers and Ollama configuration.'),('Network','Local / localhost','Model inference can be local after downloads; target URLs and installation may require network.'),('Display','Desktop browser','Used for Streamlit, and optionally headed Playwright runs.')])
p('A constrained machine should start the model and app independently, observe memory use and select an appropriate model size. The current code default differs from the narrative, so runtime verification is necessary. Use a non-production target unless automation against a live service has been explicitly approved.')
for x in [
'Environment setup should be reproducible from a clean checkout. The setup guide should name supported Python versions, install dependencies into an isolated environment, install exactly the Playwright browser binaries required, obtain the model tag and verify the Ollama endpoint. A fresh environment test catches dependencies that were installed manually but omitted from requirements.',
'Configuration should be validated at startup. An invalid base URL, unsupported browser name, negative timeout or missing model should produce a direct message. The application should show which values came from defaults and which came from persisted settings, because a stale database setting can survive a code update and confuse the user.',
'Local model performance has user-facing consequences. A prompt can take long enough that users may click a button again or assume the app has stopped. The interface should display request state, elapsed time and cancellation behavior where supported. When a request times out, it should not leave an ambiguous half-generated record.',
'Browser selection is a compatibility choice as well as a coverage choice. Chromium is a convenient default, but cross-browser regressions require running the same case in multiple engines and recording each result independently. A test passing in Chromium says nothing about Firefox or WebKit unless those browsers were actually run.',
'Test timeouts need a consistent interpretation. The Playwright engine sets a default timeout for locators, while the API engine uses a request timeout in seconds. Configuration labels should make units clear and prevent a single numeric setting from being interpreted differently across engines.',
'Headless execution is suitable for unattended runs, whereas headed mode can help a tester observe a failure. Both modes should use the same assertions. A headed run may be slower or affected by display state, so the selected mode belongs in the execution record.',
'Application lifecycle should be explicit. A run can be Pending, Running, Completed or Aborted; if a process closes unexpectedly, stale Running rows need a recovery path. On restart, the UI might identify interrupted runs and allow a user to mark them aborted without pretending that all cases completed.',
'Backup and restore should be tested, not assumed. A backup procedure should include a consistent database snapshot and all referenced files, record the application/schema version and confirm that a restored copy can open a project and inspect prior results. Copying an actively written database file may produce an inconsistent backup unless SQLite backup facilities or a quiescent window are used.'
]:p(x)
h2('3.2 Software Requirement')
