h3('3.2.1 About Python')
for x in [
'Python is an interpreted high-level language used for automation, data processing and web applications. One language can serve Streamlit, the Ollama client, JSON validation, Playwright, HTTP requests and database operations, reducing translation overhead between layers.',
'The `ai` package contains prompts, a client and response parsing. `database` defines schema and CRUD helpers. `testing` contains validation and execution engines. `pages` contains Streamlit screens. Tests import modules directly, allowing many behaviors to be checked without driving the complete interface.',
'Flexibility makes rapid development easy but increases the importance of explicit interfaces and validation. The AI layer returns decoded dictionaries and downstream code expects particular keys. The action validator is one explicit contract. Type annotations improve readability but do not replace runtime checks.',
'Reliable deployments should pin dependencies, keep secrets out of source and prompts, set operation timeouts and handle unavailable services with clear messages. Logs and exports should be reviewed for sensitive data.'
]:p(x)
h3('3.2.2 About SQLite')
for x in [
'SQLite is an embedded relational database that stores data in a file without a dedicated server. This suits a local single-user prototype: setup is simple, backups are easy to explain and Python can access it through its standard library. The database path is configured relative to the repository.',
'Connections enable foreign keys. Projects parent requirements, cases, runs and bugs. Requirement deletion can null a case reference; project deletion cascades through related data. Results connect runs and cases; screenshots and analyses may connect to results; bugs may reference a result.',
'The data helper uses parameterized values for ordinary queries and commits or rolls back through a context manager. Some update helpers interpolate column names from keyword arguments, so callers must use trusted names. Future hardening could validate column names or use explicit update functions.',
'SQLite works well for local development and small workloads. Multi-user collaboration and high-volume parallel execution would require reconsidering connection management and storage. Backups should capture the database and referenced image/report folders together; schema migrations should be versioned.'
]:p(x)

h1('4. SYSTEM DESIGN')
p('The design connects testing intent to execution evidence through a presentation layer, AI-assisted generation layer, validation and execution layer, persistence layer and reporting views. Data moves through Python dictionaries, JSON strings and SQLite rows. The architecture favors a compact local deployment and explicit review points.')
h2('4.1 Module Description')
p('The Streamlit application is organized into eleven functional pages. The main entry point configures navigation and shared project context; page modules call data helpers and AI or test services as needed.')
tab(['Module','Responsibilities'],[
('Dashboard','Project KPIs, result/severity/coverage charts and traceability view.'),('Projects','Create and manage project name, description, target base URL and status.'),('Requirements','Capture requirement fields, invoke analysis and display/store structured output.'),('AI Test Generator','Request candidate cases, support review, and generate action plans.'),('Test Cases','List, filter and edit case metadata or steps; add manual cases.'),('Test Execution','Select cases and runtime settings, execute and persist results.'),('API Testing','Configure method, endpoint, headers, body and assertions.'),('Bug Management','Create/update defects with severity, status and reproduction detail.'),('AI Failure Analysis','Classify failures and suggest root cause/investigation.'),('Reports','Build project summaries and exports.'),('Settings','Manage or display model endpoint, model, browser, mode, timeouts and screenshots.')])
p('These are functional modules in one Python runtime, not separate processes. Long model or browser operations can block interaction unless progress and cancellation are managed. The UI should prevent duplicate submission while work is active.')
p('The AI service exposes distinct methods for requirement analysis, test generation, UI actions, API actions, failure analysis, bug reports and recommendations. That separation makes prompt intent auditable. Configuration and persisted settings should be reconciled at startup so the displayed model matches the service actually used, and the active model should be stored with every analysis.')
p('The execution layer determines whether a case is API-oriented, loads action JSON, invokes the matching engine and saves the result. Playwright validates again immediately before execution. The API engine limits methods to GET, POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE and evaluates known assertion types. This keeps page code separate from low-level execution.')
for x in [
'The request-response path should be treated as a contract. Prompt templates define the requested fields; the response parser extracts JSON; schema validation checks required structure; and the consuming page decides whether the content is displayed, edited or saved. If a field is missing, the system should preserve the raw response for diagnosis but should not assume a default that changes the meaning of the requirement. Retrying is appropriate for formatting errors, but it cannot repair an incorrect interpretation by itself.',
'Temperature and context settings influence repeatability. Lower sampling variation may make similar requests more consistent, but it cannot guarantee deterministic output across model versions or runtime changes. The AI analysis table records model identity and duration, which can support comparisons if those fields are populated consistently. A future prompt-version field would make it easier to explain why an output changed after an update.',
'Action validation has two layers of responsibility. The parser-level schema checks that an action response has the expected shape. The action validator checks that each item has an allowed type and required fields, and the engine validates again before dispatch. This duplication is intentional defense in depth. Validation should be strict enough that unknown fields and unexpected types cannot create ambiguity in the executor.',
'The current action validator checks required fields and a numeric non-negative wait value. Additional semantic checks would strengthen it: cap wait durations, validate selector and value lengths, require navigation URLs to match a target allow-list, reject script-like URLs, constrain timeouts and ensure assertions use supported operators. A fixed set of action names is helpful, but parameters also influence risk.',
'Browser sessions should be isolated for each run. The engine creates a new browser context and page, which limits state carryover between separate runs. The target may still retain server-side data or cookies outside that context, so test setup and cleanup remain important. If user credentials are required, they should be stored outside generated prompts and substituted through controlled secret references.',
'The API engine creates a requests session and resolves relative endpoints using the project base URL. It handles timeout and connection errors separately, measures duration and returns a bounded response excerpt. Before using it against sensitive systems, the endpoint resolution and redirect behavior should be tested, request bodies limited, and certificates and authentication handled according to organizational policy.',
'Error categories should be stable across UI and API paths. A transport failure means the runner could not complete a request; an assertion failure means a response arrived but did not match an expectation; a validation rejection means the action definition was not permitted; and a blocked result means prerequisites are absent. These categories lead to different next steps and should remain distinguishable in charts and reports.',
'The database stores files by path rather than as blobs. This keeps the relational file smaller and makes screenshots directly viewable, but the database alone is not a complete backup. Moving the database without its screenshots and reports can leave broken references. A project export should either package referenced files or explicitly mark them as unavailable.',
'Metrics have useful but limited interpretations. A high pass rate may mean the product is stable, the cases are weak, or a recent run selected only easy cases. A low automation rate may reflect intentional manual exploration, not a gap. Open bug counts are affected by triage and status discipline. Each chart should help locate records, not replace engineering judgment.',
'Report generation should be deterministic for a selected project and run. The export should record its filter criteria and creation time so two files can be compared meaningfully. HTML must encode user-controlled fields; CSV should preserve values without formula injection; and PDF output should use consistent page layout. These controls are particularly important because AI-generated strings are also untrusted input.'
]:p(x)
h2('4.2 Input Design')
for x in [
'Inputs are captured in forms rather than requiring testers to write automation code. Projects collect name, description and base URL. Requirements have an identifier, title, description and priority. Natural language enables domain experts to contribute, but quality improves when actors, preconditions, constraints, state changes and expected outcomes are explicit.',
'AI calls receive selected fields and context. Requirement analysis takes ID, title, description and priority. Test generation can receive stored analysis. Action generation uses scenario, steps, test data, expected result and base URL. Failure analysis receives expected/actual results, error, failed step, URL, browser and timestamp.',
'API definitions use structured method, endpoint, headers, body and assertions. This is safer than a free-form command because the engine can validate the method and interpret assertion types. Supported checks include status, response time, selected JSON fields and headers.',
'Validation should occur before persistence and external interaction. Required fields should be present; URLs normalized; enumerated status and priority values constrained; JSON parsed; and actions checked against their schema. Secrets should be masked in the UI, screenshots, logs and exports. Local execution does not eliminate local exposure.',
'Review is an input-design feature. Testers should edit scenarios, remove irrelevant cases, clarify expected results and correct selectors before approval. Provenance should indicate whether a case was AI-generated or manually authored.'
]:p(x)
h2('4.3 Output Design')
for x in [
'Outputs support different decisions. Requirement analysis helps assess testability; candidate cases support coverage planning; action plans expose intended behavior; execution results compare observation to assertion; failure analysis assists triage; bug drafts create editable records; dashboards provide project-level summaries.',
'A UI result should identify case, scenario, status, duration, browser, error and failed step. URL and screenshot improve reproduction. A pass means configured actions and assertions completed; it does not prove full requirement satisfaction. Blocked cases must remain distinct from product failures.',
'API results include method, endpoint, status, duration, a bounded response excerpt, passed checks and failed checks. Excerpts may omit useful diagnostic fields. Authorization headers, tokens and personal data must not leak to views or exports.',
'Dashboard metrics aggregate stored records. Pass rate is calculated from execution results; automation rate counts cases marked Automated; open bugs include Open and In Progress; critical count groups severity. Definitions should be visible: historical repeated runs can give frequently executed cases greater weight than cases run once.',
'Reports package project context, cases, results and bugs. HTML supports browser viewing, CSV supports analysis and PDF can provide fixed layout where enabled. Exports should include creation time, selected run, status definitions and any filters. Files may contain sensitive details and need retention controls.'
]:p(x)
h2('4.4 Database Design [Table design]')
p('The schema defines eleven tables, including settings, with integer primary keys where appropriate. Human-readable requirement, case and bug IDs are stored as separate fields. Timestamps retain creation/update metadata. JSON text fields hold AI analyses and action plans. The linked structure allows project filtering and traces requirement to defect.')
