tab(['Stage','Input','Processing','Persistent output'],[
('Define','Project and requirement','Validate fields and assign identifiers','Project and requirement rows'),('Analyze','Requirement fields','Prompt, local call, JSON/schema checks','Analysis and AI record'),('Generate','Requirement and analysis','Propose structured cases','Reviewed case and steps'),('Plan','Case, steps, base URL','Propose actions and validate shape','Action JSON'),('Execute','Cases and run settings','Playwright or requests engine','Run, result and screenshot reference'),('Triage','Failed result and evidence','Optional classification and bug drafting','Analysis and editable bug'),('Report','Project records','Aggregate metrics and export','Dashboard and report files')])
p('The architecture can be represented as Tester → Streamlit → Python services → (Ollama generation; validator → Playwright; API engine) → SQLite and local files. The target application receives browser or HTTP traffic from the runner. Ollama traffic is intended for localhost, but endpoint settings can change this. Verify the endpoint and active model before sending project content; this design is not by itself a network security guarantee.')
p('The trust boundary lies between generated content and execution. JSON validation limits structure, the allow-list limits browser operations, and fixed dispatch avoids evaluating model text as code. This is not a complete sandbox. Broader deployments should restrict target hosts, limit payloads, apply timeouts, isolate browser profiles, redact secrets and review filesystem access. The current navigate action accepts a URL and should be paired with host restrictions for sensitive targets.')

for x in [
'A network-flow review should identify four paths: browser traffic to the target, API requests to the target, prompt traffic to the Ollama endpoint and report/file access on the workstation. If Ollama is bound beyond loopback or the target is remote, traffic crosses additional network boundaries. A deployment inventory should name each destination, protocol, data type and owner. This makes the phrase “runs locally” precise rather than implying that no network traffic exists.',
'Privacy controls should consider prompt retention. Requirement descriptions can reveal product plans; test data can resemble customer records; failure messages can contain identifiers; screenshots can expose names or account state. Avoid using production personal data in demonstrations. Use synthetic data and redact exports. If raw prompts are stored for debugging, access and retention should be limited.',
'Application logs should distinguish operational detail from user-facing output. Logs can record correlation ID, stage, duration and error category without copying a full request body or credential. A support workflow should let the user provide sanitized diagnostics. These choices reduce accidental exposure while retaining enough information to diagnose local setup problems.',
'The trust model includes the target application. Automated tests can submit forms, modify records or trigger emails. Even a valid allow-listed click can have significant side effects. Users should choose a test tenant, seeded database or sandbox and understand cleanup behavior before executing a suite. The action validator governs action type, not business impact.',
'Before execution, the user should confirm project, base URL, browser, headless mode and selected cases. A summary step can show whether any case sends a destructive request, uses a production URL or contains unmasked test data. Such a review screen is especially useful when AI-generated actions are approved in batches.'
]:p(x)
h1('5. SYSTEM TESTING AND IMPLEMENTATION')
p('Verification should cover individual functions, connected modules and application-level examples. The repository includes checks for parsing, validation, database helpers and metrics, plus a scripted demo workflow. The materials describe a broader demonstration in which seeded bugs are detected. This report does not claim a new test execution: historical outcomes are attributed to the supplied project narrative. A release should record environment, command, model configuration and results.')
h2('5.1 Unit Testing')
for x in [
'Unit tests isolate functions with known inputs and expected outputs. `test_ai.py` covers plain, fenced, embedded, invalid and trailing-comma JSON; schema acceptance and rejection; and action validation. Tests check that required fields are enforced and unsupported operation types are refused.',
'Database tests use temporary SQLite files to verify settings, project, requirement, test-case, step and bug operations. They check IDs, retrieved rows, counts and step ordering. Temporary databases prevent contamination of the working project data. Cleanup should remain reliable even after assertion failures.',
'Metrics tests create controlled mixes of pass/fail results and verify percentage, bug counts and empty-project behavior. These checks matter because dashboard values can silently mislead if status labels or denominators change.',
'The executor test module mainly covers validation rather than launching a browser. It checks field definitions, rejection of dangerous action names, malformed lists and mixed valid/invalid input. These are useful fast checks but do not replace a real Playwright integration run.',
'Static inventory finds 47 named test functions across four pytest modules: 17 AI, 16 database, 8 executor and 6 metrics. The supplied report narrative says 46 passing tests. Resolve this by running the current suite and recording collected and passed counts. Source inventory is not evidence that all tests pass.'
]:p(x)
tab(['Suite','Coverage','Examples'],[
('test_ai.py','Parsing, schema and actions','Embedded JSON, missing fields and disallowed actions.'),('test_database.py','CRUD and ordered steps','Project/requirement/case retrieval, step retention and bug IDs.'),('test_executor.py','Action allow-list','Required fields, shell-like labels and malformed collections.'),('test_metrics.py','Aggregate behavior','0%, 75%, 100%, bug counts, empty projects and requirements.')])
for x in [
'The response parser is a reliability boundary because language models may wrap JSON in code fences or include explanatory text. The project tests extraction from plain, fenced and embedded output and a trailing comma case. In production, parser repair should remain conservative: removing a harmless fence is different from guessing a missing assertion or inventing a required value. If the repaired structure is uncertain, fail visibly and request review.',
'Retry behavior should be bounded. The service retries schema failures up to three attempts and adds a correction instruction after invalid output. A network/runtime error returns early rather than repeatedly issuing the same failed request. This reduces wasted inference, but a retry can produce different content. The UI should indicate that a later attempt generated the displayed result and preserve enough context for diagnosis.',
'Prompt templates are part of the application’s behavior and should be version-controlled like code. Changes should be reviewed for output schema, safety language, coverage instructions and accidental inclusion of sensitive information. A prompt update should be accompanied by parser tests and representative evaluation examples so that formatting changes do not quietly break persistence.',
'Generated requirements analysis should separate facts stated by the user from assumptions suggested by the model. Useful fields include actors, preconditions, inputs, outputs, business rules, error behavior, security concerns, edge cases and open questions. Open questions should remain visible rather than being filled with plausible guesses. A reviewer can then return them to the requirement owner.',
'Generated cases should be evaluated for independence, testability and expected-result clarity. Duplicated cases inflate counts without increasing coverage. A case that says “verify the page works” is difficult to automate because it lacks an observable condition. A reviewer should remove duplication, split cases with unrelated assertions and ensure each expected result is falsifiable.',
'Generated browser actions rely on selectors. Stable application identifiers such as accessible roles, labels or deliberate test IDs are generally more maintainable than positional selectors or layout-dependent CSS. The current action model accepts selector strings, so prompt guidance and reviewer inspection are important. A selector that happens to match today can become invalid after a harmless redesign.',
'API response-time assertions need controlled expectations. Local machine load, model activity and network conditions can cause variation. A threshold should reflect a stated service-level requirement or a broad regression guard, not an arbitrary very low value. The API engine measures elapsed request time locally; it does not separate DNS, connection, server and transfer components.',
'Assertion semantics should be documented. Text matching may be case-insensitive containment unless exact mode is selected; URL assertions use containment; JSON-field paths traverse nested objects; and header checks support selected operators. A tester should know these semantics because a test’s label can imply stronger equality or normalization than the engine actually performs.',
'An execution summary should preserve both case-level and run-level status. A run may complete even when one case fails; a run can be aborted before every selected case starts; and a case can be blocked because no action plan exists. Summary logic must say whether it counts only completed results and how blocked or skipped cases affect percentages.',
'Demo validation should include both the defective and corrected behavior. For each seeded issue, run the case against the original demo to establish that it fails, apply a controlled fix or use a known-good build, and run again to establish that it passes. This positive/negative pair demonstrates test sensitivity and reduces the chance that a permanently failing test is mistaken for successful defect detection.'
]:p(x)
h2('5.2 Integration Testing')
for x in [
'Integration tests verify adjacent modules exchange data correctly. The main path crosses Streamlit, AI service, Ollama client, parser, database and display. A deterministic stub response can verify prompt wiring and persistence without a live model; a separate smoke check can confirm the configured endpoint and model are reachable.',
'Test generation should confirm requirement identifiers and analysis reach the prompt; returned cases parse; reviewer approval saves them with correct project and requirement references; and ordered steps remain intact. Malformed output, timeouts and retry exhaustion should result in a visible error and no partial case.',
'The action path should test both success and rejection. A valid plan should serialize and later pass the executor boundary. Unsupported types or missing fields should be rejected before browser launch. Testing this connection proves the boundary is invoked at the right point, beyond isolated validator tests.',
'A persistence test can create a run, execute or stub cases, store results, attach screenshots, retrieve results with case IDs, create a linked bug and verify dashboard metrics. Failure injection should cover browser startup, target connection and database errors.',
'Report integration tests should compare exported content to selected records and check escaping and sensitive-value handling. HTML should escape untrusted text; CSV must quote commas and newlines; PDF should be tested on the supported platform and checked visually.'
]:p(x)
h2('5.3 Validation Testing')
for x in [
'The Flask target is a controlled validation environment with login and registration pages, a dashboard and JSON endpoints. Its documented seeded defects cover navigation, form validation, rendered identity and authentication. A tester can compare behavior with expected outcomes using UI or API cases.',
'BUG-DEMO-001 redirects a successful login through `/home` before `/dashboard`. A test should distinguish final navigation from an intermediate redirect and define whether it checks a redirect response or the final page, avoiding false failure from a valid redirect chain.',
'BUG-DEMO-002 accepts mismatched registration password and confirmation values. A negative case should submit different values, assert a clear rejection and verify that no account was created. Unique test data avoids collisions across runs.',
'BUG-DEMO-003 displays a fixed greeting. Authenticate as a known user and assert the rendered greeting includes that identity. This checks content, not merely successful page loading.',
'BUG-DEMO-004 accepts any non-empty password for a demo account. A security case should submit a wrong password, verify denial and confirm protected content remains unavailable. Exercise this only against the local demonstration target.',
'The supplied narrative says the four bugs were detected and 46 tests passed. These are historical claims, not newly reproduced outcomes here. The checked-in tests have 47 named functions. A fresh run should record exact case IDs, environment, model and screenshots.'
]:p(x)
tab(['Defect','Test intent','Expected observation','Category'],[
('BUG-DEMO-001','Valid login; inspect final navigation','Confirm destination and distinguish redirect hop from wrong landing','Navigation'),('BUG-DEMO-002','Register with mismatched passwords','Validation appears; registration does not proceed','Input validation'),('BUG-DEMO-003','Login as named user and inspect greeting','Greeting uses authenticated name','UI/content'),('BUG-DEMO-004','Wrong non-empty password for demo account','Authentication denied; protected content unavailable','Security')])
for x in [
'A test plan should identify what will be exercised, what is excluded, the target build, environment, data setup, browser and any dependencies. If a test depends on an external service, the plan should state whether that dependency is live, stubbed or simulated. An execution record without this context may be difficult to compare with another run even when the case identifier is unchanged.',
'Test levels answer different questions. Unit checks identify defects in parsing, validation or aggregation functions. Integration checks show that data crosses module boundaries as intended. System validation exercises the running application against a controlled target. Acceptance testing confirms that stakeholders agree the behavior satisfies a business need. The current repository has meaningful unit coverage and a demo validation path; a formal acceptance process would require stakeholder-defined criteria beyond the code.',
'Test coverage should be measured against a model of behavior rather than only against lines of code or generated-case totals. For a requirement, reviewers can enumerate conditions and outcomes, then mark which case verifies each one. The existing traceability matrix is a foundation; future coverage views could show requirements with no approved case, cases without a requirement and cases that have never completed successfully.',
'Execution repeatability requires stable test data. A test that creates a user should either remove the user after the run or use a unique identifier. A test that assumes a fixed account should verify its initial state. Parallel tests must not mutate shared records in conflicting ways. The demo app’s known credentials make demonstrations convenient but should not be copied into real environments.',
'Assertions should be chosen to detect the intended failure while avoiding incidental implementation details. A URL assertion may be appropriate when route behavior is part of the requirement; otherwise a visible authenticated page may be a more stable outcome. Exact text assertions are useful for content requirements, while partial matching can accommodate harmless surrounding text. Every assertion should answer a question that matters to the requirement.',
'When a test fails, first establish whether the action ran and the target was reachable. Then inspect the failed step, URL, screenshot and logs. Compare actual behavior with the expected result and rerun only after deciding whether the environment was transient. If a failure is reproducible and contradicts an approved requirement, create a bug with minimal steps and precise data. Avoid classifying a failed locator as a product defect without inspecting the page state.',
'AI failure analysis should be assessed for calibration. A confidence label is a model-generated estimate, not a statistical probability unless calibrated against labeled data. The evidence and recommendation fields should be reviewed against the original execution. Teams can track disagreement between AI classification and human disposition to identify prompt weaknesses and categories that need better evidence.',
'The test report supplied with the project mentions 46 passing tests and four seeded defects detected. A formal validation record should include the command, date, commit, Python environment, browser, target URL, model tag and exact output. Separate automated-suite results from demo findings because they answer different questions. A test suite can pass while a particular demo scenario is not run, and a demo can expose a defect even when unit tests pass.',
'Negative security scenarios should use harmless, authorized inputs and a controlled target. The demo authentication bypass is intentionally seeded for this purpose. Broader security work should not be inferred from that example; the platform does not constitute a penetration-testing framework. Tests that can create, delete or alter data should be isolated and documented before execution.',
'Failure screenshots are useful but can contain personal information or active session data. Capture only when needed, store with restrictive permissions and apply retention. When publishing the report, replace sensitive images with redacted versions or omit them. A screenshot is evidence of one rendered state, not a complete execution trace.'
]:p(x)
h2('5.4 Test cases')
p('A useful case has a stable identifier, concise scenario, preconditions, test data, ordered steps, expected result, priority, severity and type. The schema also records automation classification and optional action JSON. These fields support execution and reporting, but scenario and expected result are the core: observed behavior must be comparable with an agreed expectation.')
p('Design begins by mapping requirements to observable outcomes. Login should cover valid and invalid credentials, empty fields, unknown users, session behavior and protected pages where specified. Registration should cover required fields, malformed values, duplicates, password rules and confirmation mismatch. APIs should include status, response structure, headers, errors, authorization and response time when required.')
p('Positive cases confirm intended use. Negative cases verify rejection of invalid or unauthorized use. Boundary cases probe limits. Validation cases inspect feedback. Security cases examine access control and unsafe input but must remain authorized and bounded. UI cases check visible state; API cases verify protocol contracts. A scenario may serve multiple categories, but its purpose should be explicit.')
p('The result vocabulary includes Not Executed, Pass, Fail, Blocked and Skipped. A blocked result means prerequisites or automation are missing; skipped means an explicit decision not to run; fail means an assertion/action did not meet its condition. Treating all non-pass states as failures distorts defect counts and pass rates.')
tab(['ID','Type','Scenario','Expected result'],[
('TC-LOGIN-001','Positive / UI','Sign in with active user and valid credentials','User reaches authenticated dashboard and sees correct identity.'),('TC-LOGIN-002','Negative / UI','Sign in with known email and incorrect password','Authentication denied; protected page unavailable.'),('TC-LOGIN-003','Validation','Submit login form with required fields blank','Missing values are identified; no authenticated session.'),('TC-REG-001','Negative / validation','Submit different password and confirmation','Registration is rejected with mismatch message.'),('TC-API-001','API / contract','Request health endpoint','Documented status and JSON health field are returned.'),('TC-API-002','API / security','Submit invalid login credentials','Specified unauthorized response and no token.'),('TC-SEC-001','Security','Request protected page without session','Denied or redirected as requirement specifies.'),('TC-BOUND-001','Boundary','Submit maximum field length and one character beyond','Valid limit accepted; out-of-range value rejected.')])
p('These are report examples, not a claim that the cases exist in the current database or have run. Adapt selectors, endpoints, setup and expectations to the target. Data should be repeatable and isolated; secrets should not be stored in plain text or exported without masking.')
for x in [
'A representative functional test starts with an approved requirement and defines the initial state, actor, data and expected outcome. The tester creates the case, reviews its steps, generates actions and checks selectors. An execution record captures the browser and target. If an assertion fails, the tester compares screenshot and actual page with the expected behavior, then classifies the outcome. If it is an application defect, the bug stores concise reproduction steps and links back to the failing result.',
'A representative API test starts with a contract statement such as “health returns status ok.” The tester chooses GET, endpoint and expected status, then adds a JSON field assertion. The engine sends the request, measures duration and returns passed/failed assertions. If the route is unreachable, the result should communicate a connection error; if the server responds with a wrong status, it should show an assertion failure. These outcomes require different corrective actions.',
'A generated case should be reviewed against the requirement before actions are generated. Reviewers can check whether the scenario covers a real user outcome, whether test data is safe, whether the expected result is supported and whether the steps are sufficiently specific. Cases with unresolved business assumptions should be edited or held rather than approved just because they parse successfully.',
'When an existing case is changed, prior execution outcomes remain relevant but may no longer describe the new case definition. A future versioning feature could record a snapshot of scenario, steps, expected result and action JSON with each execution. Until then, teams should avoid rewriting a case in a way that obscures what an earlier run actually tested.',
'A useful run review ends with a decision: accept the result, correct the automation, retry an environment failure, or create a defect. The dashboard can summarize the run, but the decision should be based on case-level evidence. Recording the disposition helps teams avoid counting a selector repair as a product fix or closing a bug without retesting.'
]:p(x)
h2('5.5 Implementation and maintenance')
for x in [
'The application runs as a local Streamlit process. Ollama runs separately and serves the configured model; Flask can run on its own port; SQLite is initialized from the schema; Playwright browser binaries are installed through its setup command. These independent services need clear status messages so users can identify a missing prerequisite.',
'Configuration is split between `config.py` constants and persisted SQLite settings. The code and database both seed Qwen2.5-Coder 7B, while users can change the active model through settings. A robust startup should display the active tag and record the exact model with each analysis. Silent substitution should be avoided.',
'Maintenance includes dependency updates, model compatibility checks, database backups, pruning old files, prompt review and rerunning tests after changes. New action types require coordinated updates to allow-list, field schema, executor, prompts and tests. Database changes need migrations. Export formats require escaping and confidentiality review.',
'Operations should distinguish model unavailability from invalid output, browser startup from product failure, and target connection from contract failure. AI classification is advisory because the model sees only supplied evidence and can misread it.',
'Security maintenance includes target-host restrictions, credential handling, prompt/screenshot retention, file permissions and export review. The allow-list prevents unsupported action labels, but navigation accepts a URL and should be constrained for sensitive deployments.',
'A release manifest should record revision, Python/dependency versions, model tag, OS, browser version, test command and exact result counts, demo revision and date. This turns “tests pass” into reproducible evidence.'
]:p(x)

h1('6. CONCLUSION AND FUTURE ENHANCEMENT')
h2('6.1 Conclusion')
for x in [
'AI Software Tester demonstrates an integrated approach to local AI-assisted QA. It connects requirement analysis, candidate test design, automation planning, browser/API execution, result persistence, failure interpretation, bug drafting, dashboard metrics and reporting. Established components and a compact relational model keep the project understandable and runnable on a workstation.',
'The safety design treats model output as structured data that passes parsing, schema checks and action validation. The model does not receive a general-purpose code execution interface through the defined browser actions. A second validation in the Playwright engine rejects malformed or unsupported actions before the browser processes them. These controls reduce one risk class; URL scope, credentials, target authorization and retention still require deployment controls.',
'The demo application supplies testable navigation, validation, UI-content and authentication behaviors. The data model connects requirements, cases, runs, results and bugs. Local inference and storage can help where external data transfer is undesirable, although the model and browser still need installation and management.',
'The repository narrative should be reconciled before formal claims: the source has 47 named test functions while an existing narrative says 46 passed. A fresh suite run can settle the collected and passed counts. The database schema contains eleven tables including settings. Reproducibility also requires recording the active model and runtime configuration.',
'The project is a useful prototype for exploring how local language models can reduce repetitive QA work while keeping testers in control. Its strongest contribution is a connected lifecycle and explicit execution boundary. Further work should emphasize provenance, environment controls and repeatable evidence before broadening generated behavior.'
]:p(x)
h2('6.2 Future Enhancement')
for a,b in [
('Multiple model profiles and comparison','Compare completeness, correctness and stability across approved local models. Record model/version, prompt version, options and timing for reproducibility.'),
('CI/CD integration','Add a command-line entry point or API for approved suites, machine-readable exit codes and artifact export. Use protected secrets and explicit target allow-lists.'),
('Multi-user access and roles','Add authentication, permissions, audit history and concurrency rules before shared project editing.'),
('Visual regression','Capture baselines and compare screenshots with configurable thresholds and masks for dynamic regions; require human review of diffs.'),
('OpenAPI and contract testing','Import schemas, generate candidate cases, validate responses and report contract changes without exposing credentials to prompts.'),
('Safe action registry','Version action definitions, validation and executor implementations. Extensions should declare permissions, target constraints and tests; arbitrary evaluation should remain excluded.'),
('Traceability queries','Find requirements without approved cases, unexecuted cases, recurring failures, stale tests and unresolved severe bugs. Link aggregates to records.'),
('Observability','Store structured logs, traces, network summaries and model latency with configurable retention and correlation IDs.'),
('Migration and archival','Version schemas, add archival and safe project export/import, and include referenced files when packaging.'),
('Resilience and scale','Move long operations to workers with cancellation and queue limits; consider server persistence only if concurrent workload warrants it.'),
('Accessibility','Audit keyboard access, labels, contrast and status communication. Distinguish product failures from setup errors.'),
('Evaluation dataset','Measure coverage, unsupported assumptions, valid actions and human edits on curated requirements across model versions.')]:h3(a);p(b)
p('Prioritize by audience. A single-user prototype should first reconcile configuration and produce repeatable evidence. A team service also needs authentication, audit history, concurrency control and a shared persistence layer.')

for x in [
'An implementation roadmap should sequence improvements by risk and dependency. First, reconcile the model setting and display the exact active model. Next, record fresh test results and align the documented suite count. Then add target-host restrictions and improve error categories. These steps strengthen trust in existing behavior before the project adds team collaboration or more action types.',
'The second stage can improve evidence quality. Persist prompt version, model tag and case definition at execution time; provide export manifests; add run recovery for interrupted processes; and verify backups. These changes make historical results interpretable and lower the chance that a team will compare runs produced under different settings as if they were equivalent.',
'A later stage can introduce asynchronous execution and CI support. A command-line runner should accept a project or exported suite, selected browser and environment variables, and should emit a stable machine-readable summary. It should return a failing process code when required assertions fail, but distinguish configuration errors from product failures. CI integration should preserve screenshots and logs as build artifacts.',
'Multi-user features require broader architectural work than adding a login form. The system needs a shared service boundary, authorization checks on every project and file, audit events, concurrent identifier generation, conflict handling and database migration. The storage layer may need a server database. User roles should be designed around actual responsibilities and reviewed for data isolation.',
'Model evaluation should compare outputs on a fixed requirement set. Reviewers can label expected scenarios and measure useful coverage, unsupported assumptions, duplicate rate, parse success and action validity. Human edit distance can show how much correction is required. These measures should be reported alongside latency and resource use, and should not be collapsed into one score without a clear rationale.',
'An organization adopting the platform should begin with a low-risk internal application and synthetic data. The team can inspect generated cases, estimate review effort, measure failure triage quality and note environment setup costs. Only after repeatability and access controls are established should the platform run against higher-impact systems.',
'Project success can be judged by whether testers spend less time on repetitive translation while retaining or improving requirement coverage and failure reproducibility. A before-and-after comparison should use comparable requirements and include human review time, duplicated cases, defect signal quality and maintenance work. Faster generation alone may shift effort into correction rather than eliminate it.',
'The report’s recommendations are intentionally staged. The prototype already demonstrates a useful local workflow. Enhancements should preserve simplicity where it is valuable, but should not claim enterprise scale until concurrency, permissions, migration, recovery and audit behavior are implemented and verified.'
]:p(x)
